Read the first and last row of each block. That slope, not the level, is
the contamination: how much a procedure's false-alarm rate on innocent
items is driven by how many guilty items are in the questionnaire with
them. It is the difference between a test of one item and a test of the
company that item keeps.

  all_other  shift 0.2: 0.000 with no DIF present -> 0.230 with 4 items carrying it (+0.230)
  all_other  shift 0.4: 0.000 with no DIF present -> 0.850 with 4 items carrying it (+0.850)
  all_other  shift 0.8: 0.000 with no DIF present -> 1.000 with 4 items carrying it (+1.000)
  anchored   shift 0.2: 0.010 with no DIF present -> 0.083 with 4 items carrying it (+0.073)
  anchored   shift 0.4: 0.010 with no DIF present -> 0.325 with 4 items carrying it (+0.315)
  anchored   shift 0.8: 0.010 with no DIF present -> 0.300 with 4 items carrying it (+0.290)
  lordif     shift 0.2: 0.039 with no DIF present -> 0.100 with 4 items carrying it (+0.061)
  lordif     shift 0.4: 0.039 with no DIF present -> 0.750 with 4 items carrying it (+0.711)
  lordif     shift 0.8: 0.039 with no DIF present -> 1.000 with 4 items carrying it (+0.961)

The purified row is not monotone in the bias size, which is not noise:
purification spots a guilty item more easily the larger its bias, so at
the widest shift fewer guilty items survive into the anchor. The
guilty_in_anchor figure below is the direct evidence for that reading.

Reading across a row rather than down a column: at a bias size the
procedure can barely detect, contamination is mild however many items
carry it. The anchor breaks down when the bias is both widespread AND
large enough to see, which is exactly the regime a real questionnaire with
a systematic group difference sits in.

And purification is not free. With 4 guilty items, the average number of
them that ended up INSIDE the anchor set, never tested and instead defining
the scale every other item is judged against:
  shift 0.2: 1.05 of 4 guilty items became anchors
  shift 0.4: 1.05 of 4 guilty items became anchors
  shift 0.8: 0.90 of 4 guilty items became anchors

